body:
Scaffold refresh: the first half of `kalide upgrade`. Kalide-owned scaffold content is the content kalide itself writes into a project and still owns — the two embedded `AGENTS.md` author guides (the deck guide, agent-authoring, and the library guide, library-agent-guide) and the minimal `kalide init` seed tree (cli-init-minimal-seed: the seed `templates/library.yaml`, the `hello` slide template with its `example.md`, the default theme, and the starter slide `slides/1-hello.md`). Today these are written once and never refreshed — every scaffold writer writes the `AGENTS.md` guide only when none exists (writeHelloSeed in internal/scaffold/helloseed.go, InitExternal in external.go, InitLibrary in library.go) — so a project scaffolded by an older kalide keeps that release's guide after the binary is upgraded, and the guides have already gone stale once when the format evolved (e.g. the reserved `media:` theme-CSS prefix, theme-media-references, had to be added to both). Upgrade is the runtime counterpart of the build-time agent-guide-drift-check.

A file in the refresh set is written only when it is (a) absent, or (b) present and byte-identical to a known released kalide version of that same file (global.constraint.upgrade-known-version-catalog). A present file whose bytes match no known released version — because the author edited it, or because it is not a kalide scaffold file — is treated as author content: it is left byte-for-byte untouched and reported, never overwritten, whatever the format migration would otherwise do. The set is scoped to the project's scaffold form and never grows: a no-arg seed deck owns the whole seed tree; an external-library deck (`templates:` configured) never owned a local `templates/` or a starter slide, so upgrade must not create one; a library owns its library-root guide and its seeded default theme, never templates or media. `kalide.yaml` is the deck config the author edits (title, author, date, properties) and is not a whole-file refresh candidate; its structure is migration territory, not refresh territory. Every refreshed and every left-alone path is reported.

AC:
- a deck scaffolded by a previous kalide with an unmodified old `AGENTS.md` ends with the current guide after upgrade, and the project still serves (agent-authoring);
- an author-edited `AGENTS.md` is left byte-for-byte unchanged and reported;
- an external-library deck is not given a local `templates/` directory or a starter slide it never had;
- an owned seed file the author never touched is refreshed to the current content; the same file with author edits is left untouched and reported;
- author slides, sections, templates, themes and media are byte-for-byte identical before and after;
- the command output lists every refreshed path and every path left alone.
